ITEMS = [
    # ---------------- Basics ----------------
    Q('what-playwright', 'Basics', 'e',
      'What is Playwright, and which languages and browsers does it support?',
      'Think of who builds it, the four official languages and the three browser engines.',
      'Playwright is an open source browser automation library from Microsoft. It has official versions for TypeScript and JavaScript, Python, Java and .NET. It drives Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, the engine behind Safari, on Windows, Linux and macOS, headless or headed, and it can also use branded Chrome and Edge.',
      '<p>For TypeScript and JavaScript there is also Playwright Test, a full test runner with fixtures, parallel workers, retries, projects and reporters. In Python teams usually run Playwright through the pytest-playwright plugin, and in Java through JUnit or TestNG.</p><p>There is no official version for Go, C or C++. A community project, playwright-go, exists for Go.</p>',
      points=['Microsoft, open source', 'TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Java, .NET', 'Chromium, Firefox, WebKit'],
      follow=['Why would a team pick the Java version over TypeScript?']),
    Q('vs-selenium', 'Basics', 'm',
      'How is Playwright different from Selenium WebDriver?',
      'Compare how each one talks to the browser and what each one does for waiting.',
      'Selenium sends each command as a separate HTTP request to a driver such as chromedriver, which then controls the browser. Playwright keeps one connection open to the browser and sends commands and events over it. Playwright also waits for elements to be ready before acting, retries assertions until they pass, gives every test a fresh browser context, and ships tools such as codegen, the trace viewer and network mocking out of the box.',
      '<p>Selenium follows the W3C WebDriver standard and supports more browsers and older versions, and Selenium Grid is widely used. Playwright tests patched builds of Firefox and WebKit that ship with each Playwright version, so a Playwright version and its browsers always match.</p>',
      points=['One connection against one HTTP call per command', 'Auto waiting and retrying assertions', 'Browser context per test', 'Trace viewer, codegen and network mocking built in'],
      follow=['Have you migrated Selenium tests to Playwright? What changed in the waits?']),
    Q('vs-cypress', 'Basics', 'm',
      'How is Playwright different from Cypress?',
      'Where does each tool run its code, and what limits does that bring?',
      'Cypress runs the test code inside the browser, next to the app. Playwright runs the test code in its own process and controls the browser from outside. That lets Playwright handle several tabs, several browser contexts, other origins and iframes in one test, test WebKit, and write tests in four languages, while Cypress tests are JavaScript or TypeScript only.',
      '<p>Cypress has a well known interactive runner and time travel debugging. Playwright gives similar insight through UI mode and the trace viewer.</p>'),
    Q('architecture', 'Basics', 'm',
      'Explain browser, browser context and page in Playwright.',
      'Which one is expensive to start and which one gives isolation?',
      'A browser is one running browser process, which is slow to start. A browser context is an isolated session inside that browser, with its own cookies, local storage and cache, much like a fresh incognito profile, and it is fast to create. A page is one tab inside a context. Playwright Test starts one browser per worker and gives each test a new context and page.',
      '<p>Two contexts in one test let you check two users at once, such as a buyer and a seller in a chat. In Python <code>browser.new_context()</code> and <code>context.new_page()</code>, in Java <code>browser.newContext()</code> and <code>context.newPage()</code>.</p>',
      points=['Browser: process, slow to start', 'Context: isolated session, cheap', 'Page: one tab'],
      follow=['How would you test a chat between two users in one test?']),
    Q('install', 'Basics', 'e',
      'How do you install Playwright in TypeScript, Python and Java?',
      'Two steps in each language: the library, then the browsers.',
      'TypeScript or JavaScript: <code>npm init playwright@latest</code>, which adds @playwright/test, a config file and an example test, then <code>npx playwright install</code>. Python: <code>pip install pytest-playwright</code> then <code>playwright install</code>. Java: add the com.microsoft.playwright dependency to pom.xml, and the browsers download the first time Playwright runs.',
      '<p>On Linux CI machines add <code>--with-deps</code> so the system libraries the browsers need are installed too. Each Playwright version expects its own browser builds, so update both together.</p>'),
    Q('codegen', 'Basics', 'e',
      'What is codegen, and should you commit its output as is?',
      'It records clicks into code. Think about what a framework adds.',
      '<code>npx playwright codegen &lt;url&gt;</code> opens a browser and the Inspector, and writes code for each click and fill in the language you pick. It is useful for finding good locators quickly. Its output is a flat script, so a team moves the locators into page objects, replaces hard coded data, and adds assertions before committing it.',
      '<p>Python runs it as <code>playwright codegen</code>, Java as <code>mvn exec:java -e -D exec.mainClass=com.microsoft.playwright.CLI -D exec.args="codegen demo.playwright.dev"</code>.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Locators ----------------
    Q('locator-priority', 'Locators', 'e',
      'Which locators does Playwright recommend, and in what order?',
      'Start from what a user or a screen reader sees.',
      'User facing locators first: <code>getByRole</code> with the accessible name, then <code>getByLabel</code>, <code>getByPlaceholder</code>, <code>getByText</code>, <code>getByAltText</code> and <code>getByTitle</code>. Use <code>getByTestId</code> when the team adds test ids. CSS and XPath are the last choice, because they break when the page structure changes.',
      '<p>In Python the names are snake case, such as <code>page.get_by_role("button", name="Sign in")</code>. Java uses <code>page.getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON, new Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Sign in"))</code>.</p>',
      acode="await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();\nawait page.getByLabel('Email').fill('asha@shop.test');\nawait expect(page.getByTestId('cart-count')).toHaveText('2');",
      points=['getByRole first', 'Labels, placeholders and text next', 'Test ids when agreed with developers', 'CSS and XPath last']),
    Q('strict-mode', 'Locators', 'm',
      'What is strict mode, and what happens when a locator matches three elements?',
      'An action needs exactly one target.',
      'Locators are strict. An action such as click or fill on a locator that matches more than one element throws an error that lists the matches, instead of clicking the first one. You fix it by making the locator more specific, filtering it, or choosing one on purpose with <code>first()</code>, <code>last()</code> or <code>nth()</code>.',
      '<p>Assertions about many elements, such as <code>toHaveCount</code>, are allowed on a locator that matches several. Picking <code>first()</code> just to silence the error hides a real problem, since the page may change the order.</p>'),
    Q('filter-chain', 'Locators', 'm',
      'How do you click "Add to cart" on the card for one product when every card has the same button?',
      'Narrow to the right card first, then look inside it.',
      'Find the card that contains the product name with <code>filter({ hasText })</code> or <code>filter({ has })</code>, then find the button inside that card.',
      '<p>Python: <code>page.get_by_test_id("product").filter(has_text="Desk lamp").get_by_role("button", name="Add to cart").click()</code>. Java: <code>page.getByTestId("product").filter(new Locator.FilterOptions().setHasText("Desk lamp")).getByRole(AriaRole.BUTTON, ...)</code>.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode="await page.getByTestId('product')\n  .filter({ hasText: 'Desk lamp' })\n  .getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to cart' })\n  .click();"),
    Q('locator-vs-handle', 'Locators', 'h',
      'What is the difference between a Locator and an ElementHandle?',
      'One of them finds the element again every time you use it.',
      'A Locator is a description of how to find an element. It finds the element again each time you act or assert, so it keeps working when the page re-renders. An ElementHandle points at one DOM node found once, and it goes stale if that node is replaced. The docs discourage element handles and recommend locators.',
      '<p>This is why you can create locators in a page object constructor before the page has even loaded. Nothing is searched until an action or assertion runs.</p>'),
    Q('frames-shadow', 'Locators', 'm',
      'How do you work with iframes and shadow DOM?',
      'One needs a special locator, the other mostly works on its own.',
      'For an iframe use <code>page.frameLocator("#payment")</code> or <code>page.locator("iframe").contentFrame()</code>, then locate inside it as usual. Open shadow DOM needs nothing special, because Playwright locators look inside open shadow roots by default. The exception is XPath, which does not go into shadow roots.',
      '<p>Python: <code>page.frame_locator("#payment").get_by_label("Card number")</code>. Java: <code>page.frameLocator("#payment").getByLabel("Card number")</code>.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Waiting and assertions ----------------
    Q('auto-wait', 'Waiting and assertions', 'e',
      'What is auto waiting? Which checks run before a click?',
      'Playwright checks the element is ready before acting.',
      'Before an action Playwright waits until the element passes actionability checks. For a click it must be attached to the page, visible, stable (not moving), able to receive events (not covered by another element) and enabled. Fill also needs the element to be editable. If the checks do not pass within the timeout, the action fails with a message that says which check failed.',
      '<p>The default wait is the action timeout. Some actions skip checks that do not apply, for example <code>fill</code> does not check that the element receives events. <code>force: true</code> skips the checks, which hides real problems and is rarely the right fix.</p>',
      points=['Attached and visible', 'Stable, not animating', 'Receives events, not covered', 'Enabled, and editable for fill']),
    Q('web-first', 'Waiting and assertions', 'm',
      'What are web first assertions? Why is <code>expect(locator).toHaveText()</code> better than reading the text and comparing it?',
      'One of them retries.',
      'Web first assertions take a locator and retry until the condition is true or the expect timeout runs out, which is 5 seconds by default. Reading the text with <code>textContent()</code> and comparing it checks once, so it fails if the page has not updated yet.',
      '<p>Python: <code>expect(page.get_by_test_id("total")).to_have_text("₹1,299")</code>. Java: <code>assertThat(page.getByTestId("total")).hasText("₹1,299")</code> with a static import of PlaywrightAssertions.</p>',
      code="// flaky\nexpect(await page.getByTestId('total').textContent()).toBe('₹1,299');\n// waits and retries\nawait expect(page.getByTestId('total')).toHaveText('₹1,299');"),
    Q('timeouts', 'Waiting and assertions', 'h',
      'Name the timeouts in Playwright and their defaults.',
      'There is one for the test, one for expect, and settings for actions and navigation.',
      'In Playwright Test the test timeout is 30 seconds and the expect timeout is 5 seconds. Actions and navigations have no timeout of their own by default, so the test timeout limits them, and you can set <code>actionTimeout</code> and <code>navigationTimeout</code> in config. In the Python and Java libraries actions wait 30 seconds by default, which <code>setDefaultTimeout</code> changes, and assertions wait 5 seconds.',
      '<p>Raising timeouts hides slowness. Find out why the page is slow first, then raise only the one timeout that needs it.</p>'),
    Q('wait-timeout', 'Waiting and assertions', 'm',
      'A teammate added <code>page.waitForTimeout(5000)</code> to fix a flaky test. What do you say?',
      'It either wastes time or is still too short.',
      'A fixed sleep waits the full 5 seconds on a fast run and can still be too short on a slow one, so the test stays flaky and gets slower. Wait for the thing the test needs instead: a web first assertion on the element, <code>page.waitForResponse</code> for an API call, or <code>page.waitForURL</code> for a navigation.',
      '<p>The docs say <code>waitForTimeout</code> should only be used for debugging. The same applies to <code>networkidle</code>, which the docs mark as discouraged because it waits for a quiet network rather than for what the test checks.</p>',
      kind='Scenario'),
    Q('soft-poll', 'Waiting and assertions', 'h',
      'What are <code>expect.soft</code>, <code>expect.poll</code> and <code>toPass</code>?',
      'One keeps going after a failure, two retry something that is not a locator.',
      '<code>expect.soft</code> records a failure but lets the test continue, and the test is still marked failed at the end. <code>expect.poll</code> calls a function again and again until its return value matches, which suits API checks. <code>expect(async () =&gt; {...}).toPass()</code> retries a whole block of code until it stops throwing.',
      '<p>These are Playwright Test features in TypeScript and JavaScript. Python and Java teams use their runner for soft checks, such as TestNG SoftAssert, and write their own retry loop for polling.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Pages, events and network ----------------
    Q('new-tab', 'Pages and events', 'm',
      'A click opens a new tab. How do you get hold of that tab?',
      'Start waiting for the event before the click.',
      'Start waiting for the new page before the click, then click, then use the page the wait returns.',
      '<p>Python: <code>with context.expect_page() as info: page.get_by_text("Help").click()</code> then <code>new_page = info.value</code>. Java: <code>Page help = context.waitForPage(() -&gt; page.getByText("Help").click())</code>. The same pattern works for downloads, popups and responses.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode="const pagePromise = context.waitForEvent('page');\nawait page.getByText('Help').click();\nconst help = await pagePromise;\nawait expect(help).toHaveTitle(/Help/);"),
    Q('dialogs', 'Pages and events', 'm',
      'What does Playwright do with an alert or confirm dialog by default, and how do you accept one?',
      'If nobody listens, Playwright makes a choice for you.',
      'By default Playwright dismisses dialogs, so a confirm returns false. To accept one, register a listener before the action that opens it, for example <code>page.once("dialog", d =&gt; d.accept())</code>.',
      '<p>Python: <code>page.once("dialog", lambda d: d.accept())</code>. Java: <code>page.onceDialog(d -&gt; d.accept())</code>. A listener must accept or dismiss the dialog, otherwise the page waits.</p>'),
    Q('upload-download', 'Pages and events', 'e',
      'How do you upload a file and check a download?',
      'One sets files on an input. The other is an event.',
      'Upload with <code>setInputFiles</code> on the file input, which needs no real file dialog. For a download, wait for the download event around the click, then read <code>suggestedFilename()</code> and save it with <code>saveAs</code>.',
      '<p>Python: <code>with page.expect_download() as info: ...</code> then <code>info.value.save_as(path)</code>. Java: <code>Download d = page.waitForDownload(() -&gt; page.getByRole(...).click())</code>. A download is deleted when its browser context closes, so save it first.</p>',
      acode="await page.getByLabel('Invoice').setInputFiles('data/invoice.pdf');\n\nconst downloadPromise = page.waitForEvent('download');\nawait page.getByRole('link', { name: 'Export CSV' }).click();\nconst download = await downloadPromise;\nexpect(download.suggestedFilename()).toBe('orders.csv');"),
    Q('route', 'Network and API', 'm',
      'How do you mock an API response in a UI test? When would you do it?',
      'route lets you fulfill, abort or change a request.',
      'Use <code>page.route</code> with a URL pattern. In the handler call <code>route.fulfill</code> with your own status and body, <code>route.abort</code> to fail the request, or <code>route.continue</code> to let it through, optionally changed. Mock when the real response is hard to produce, such as an empty list, a 500 error or a slow service.',
      '<p>Keep most tests on the real backend, since mocks can drift from what the API really returns. Python: <code>page.route("**/api/products", lambda r: r.fulfill(status=500))</code>. Java: <code>page.route("**/api/products", r -&gt; r.fulfill(new Route.FulfillOptions().setStatus(500)))</code>.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode="await page.route('**/api/products', route =>\n  route.fulfill({ status: 200, json: [] }));\nawait page.goto('/products');\nawait expect(page.getByText('No products match your search')).toBeVisible();"),
    Q('api-testing', 'Network and API', 'm',
      'Can Playwright test APIs? How do you use the API to speed up UI tests?',
      'There is a request context that shares cookies with the browser.',
      'Yes. APIRequestContext sends HTTP requests without a browser: the <code>request</code> fixture in Playwright Test, <code>playwright.request.new_context()</code> in Python and <code>playwright.request().newContext()</code> in Java. UI tests use it to create data, such as an order or a user, through the API in a fraction of the time the UI would take, then check only the screen under test.',
      '<p><code>page.request</code> and <code>context.request</code> share cookies with the browser context, so an API login also logs the page in.</p>'),
    Q('wait-response', 'Network and API', 'h',
      'How do you check that clicking "Place order" sends the right request?',
      'Wait for the request or response around the click, then inspect it.',
      'Start <code>page.waitForResponse</code> (or <code>waitForRequest</code>) with a URL or predicate before the click, then check the status and the JSON body after it.',
      '<p>Starting the wait after the click can miss a fast response, so create the promise first and await it after. Python uses <code>with page.expect_response(...) as info</code> and Java uses <code>page.waitForResponse(predicate, () -&gt; ...)</code>.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode="const responsePromise = page.waitForResponse(r =>\n  r.url().endsWith('/api/orders') && r.request().method() === 'POST');\nawait page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Place order' }).click();\nconst response = await responsePromise;\nexpect(response.status()).toBe(201);\nexpect((await response.json()).items).toHaveLength(2);"),

    # ---------------- Fixtures and hooks ----------------
    Q('fixtures-ts', 'Fixtures and hooks', 'm',
      'What is a fixture in Playwright Test, and how do you write your own?',
      'test.extend, then use, then cleanup after use.',
      'A fixture is a value a test asks for by name, such as <code>page</code> or <code>request</code>. Playwright Test creates it only when a test needs it and cleans it up after. You add your own with <code>test.extend</code>: code before <code>await use(value)</code> is set up and code after it is teardown.',
      '<p>Fixtures have test scope by default and can have worker scope for expensive things. Setting <code>auto: true</code> runs a fixture for every test even if no test names it. Compared with beforeEach hooks, fixtures are reusable, lazy and typed.</p>',
      kind='Coding',
      acode="import { test as base } from '@playwright/test';\nimport { ProductsPage } from './pages/products-page';\n\nexport const test = base.extend<{ products: ProductsPage }>({\n  products: async ({ page }, use) => {\n    const products = new ProductsPage(page);\n    await products.open();\n    await use(products);\n  },\n});"),
    Q('fixtures-py', 'Fixtures and hooks', 'm',
      'How do fixtures work with pytest-playwright?',
      'The plugin gives you page, context and browser. conftest.py holds your own.',
      'pytest-playwright provides fixtures such as <code>page</code>, <code>context</code>, <code>browser</code> and <code>playwright</code>. A test asks for them as arguments. Your own fixtures go in conftest.py, with <code>yield</code> separating setup from teardown, and you can override <code>browser_context_args</code> to change context options such as the base URL or viewport.',
      '<p>Command line options such as <code>--headed</code>, <code>--browser firefox</code>, <code>--base-url</code>, <code>--tracing retain-on-failure</code> and <code>--screenshot only-on-failure</code> come from the plugin.</p>',
      lang='py',
      acode='import pytest\nfrom framework.pages.products_page import ProductsPage\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef products(page):\n    products = ProductsPage(page)\n    products.open()\n    yield products'),
    Q('java-setup', 'Fixtures and hooks', 'm',
      'How do you manage the browser in a Java framework with TestNG?',
      'Think of which annotation starts what, and threads.',
      'A base test class starts Playwright and the browser once, for example in <code>@BeforeClass</code>, creates a new context and page in <code>@BeforeMethod</code>, and closes the context in <code>@AfterMethod</code>. For parallel runs each thread needs its own Playwright object, often kept in a ThreadLocal, because Playwright Java is not thread safe.',
      '<p>With JUnit 5 the <code>@UsePlaywright</code> annotation can inject <code>Page</code> and other objects into test methods, with options from an <code>OptionsFactory</code>. The Playwright docs state that all calls on a Playwright object and the objects it creates must happen on the thread that created it.</p>',
      lang='java',
      acode='public class BaseTest {\n    protected Playwright playwright;\n    protected Browser browser;\n    protected BrowserContext context;\n    protected Page page;\n\n    @BeforeClass\n    public void startBrowser() {\n        playwright = Playwright.create();\n        browser = playwright.chromium().launch();\n    }\n\n    @BeforeMethod\n    public void openPage() {\n        context = browser.newContext();\n        page = context.newPage();\n    }\n\n    @AfterMethod(alwaysRun = true)\n    public void closePage() {\n        context.close();\n    }\n\n    @AfterClass(alwaysRun = true)\n    public void stopBrowser() {\n        playwright.close();\n    }\n}'),
    Q('hooks-vs-fixtures', 'Fixtures and hooks', 'h',
      'In Playwright Test, when would you still use <code>beforeEach</code> instead of a fixture?',
      'Think of set up that only one file needs.',
      'Use a hook for short set up that belongs to one file or one describe block, such as opening a page every test in that file starts from. Use a fixture when the set up is shared across files, needs cleanup, or should run only for the tests that ask for it.',
      '<p><code>beforeAll</code> can only use worker scoped fixtures such as <code>browser</code>, not the test <code>page</code>. Avoid it for UI state, since tests then depend on each other.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Framework design ----------------
    Q('pom', 'Framework design', 'e',
      'What is the Page Object Model, and what should a page object not contain?',
      'Locators and actions go in. One thing usually stays in the test.',
      'A page object is a class for one screen that keeps its locators and the actions a user does there, such as <code>login(user)</code>. Tests call these methods and read like steps. Assertions usually stay in the test, so the page object can be reused by tests that expect different results.',
      '<p>Components that appear on many pages, such as a header or a cart badge, get their own class that page objects use.</p>',
      acode="export class LoginPage {\n  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}\n  readonly email = this.page.getByLabel('Email');\n  readonly password = this.page.getByLabel('Password');\n\n  async login(user: User) {\n    await this.page.goto('/login');\n    await this.email.fill(user.email);\n    await this.password.fill(user.password);\n    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();\n  }\n}"),
    Q('folder-structure', 'Framework design', 'm',
      'Describe the folder structure of a Playwright framework you would build.',
      'Separate framework code from tests, and settings from data.',
      'Tests sit in their own folder, grouped by feature, and hold no locators. Framework code holds pages, components, fixtures, API helpers, config per environment and test data. The root has the runner config (playwright.config.ts, pytest.ini with conftest.py, or pom.xml with testng.xml), a .env.example for settings and the CI workflow under .github/workflows.',
      '<p>In Java, Maven expects framework code in src/main/java and tests in src/test/java. In Python the framework is a package next to the tests folder.</p>',
      kind='Design', acode='shop-tests/\n  .github/workflows/playwright.yml\n  src/\n    api/          API helpers for fast set up\n    components/   header, cart badge\n    config/       env.ts reads .env\n    data/         users and products\n    fixtures/     test.extend\n    pages/        one class per screen\n  tests/\n    cart/  checkout/  login/\n  .env.example\n  playwright.config.ts\n  package.json'),
    Q('storage-state', 'Framework design', 'm',
      'How do you avoid logging in through the UI in every test?',
      'Save the logged in state once and load it into new contexts.',
      'Log in once in a setup project or global setup, save the cookies and local storage with <code>context.storageState({ path })</code>, and start each test context with that file through the <code>storageState</code> option. In config the setup project is listed in the <code>dependencies</code> of the browser projects so it runs first.',
      '<p>Python: <code>context.storage_state(path="auth.json")</code> and <code>browser.new_context(storage_state="auth.json")</code>. Java: <code>context.storageState(new BrowserContext.StorageStateOptions().setPath(Paths.get("auth.json")))</code>. Keep the file out of Git, since it holds a session.</p>'),
    Q('config-env', 'Framework design', 'm',
      'How do you run the same suite against QA and staging?',
      'Nothing about the environment should be hard coded.',
      'Read the base URL and credentials from environment variables, often loaded from a .env file locally and from pipeline secrets in CI, and set <code>baseURL</code> from them so tests use relative paths such as <code>page.goto("/login")</code>. Java frameworks often read <code>System.getProperty("env")</code> and a properties file per environment, and Python uses <code>--base-url</code> or an environment variable.',
      '<p>Commit a .env.example with the names but no real values, and never commit passwords.</p>'),
    Q('test-data', 'Framework design', 'h',
      'Two tests running in parallel both edit the same user and fail at random. How do you fix it?',
      'Shared data is the problem, not the timing.',
      'Give each test its own data. Create a fresh user or order through the API in a fixture, with a unique name such as one with the worker index or a timestamp, and delete it after the test. If a shared account is unavoidable, make the tests that change it run serially, but treat that as a last resort.',
      '<p><code>testInfo.workerIndex</code> in Playwright Test and the <code>worker_id</code> fixture of pytest-xdist help build names that do not clash.</p>',
      kind='Scenario'),

    # ---------------- Debugging and reports ----------------
    Q('trace', 'Debugging and reports', 'e',
      'What is a trace, and how do you turn it on?',
      'A file that records every action with the page at each step.',
      'A trace is a zip file with every action, a DOM snapshot before and after each one, screenshots, console messages and network calls. You open it with <code>npx playwright show-trace trace.zip</code> or at trace.playwright.dev. In Playwright Test set <code>trace: "on-first-retry"</code> or <code>"retain-on-failure"</code> in config. Python uses <code>--tracing retain-on-failure</code>. Java calls <code>context.tracing().start(...)</code> and <code>stop(...)</code> with a path.',
      '<p>A trace from a CI failure is usually enough to find the cause without running the test again on your machine.</p>',
      lang='java',
      acode='context.tracing().start(new Tracing.StartOptions()\n    .setScreenshots(true).setSnapshots(true).setSources(true));\n// ... test steps ...\ncontext.tracing().stop(new Tracing.StopOptions()\n    .setPath(Paths.get("traces/checkout.zip")));'),
    Q('debug-tools', 'Debugging and reports', 'e',
      'Which tools do you use to debug a failing test locally?',
      'Inspector, UI mode, headed runs and a pause.',
      'Run with <code>--debug</code> to open the Playwright Inspector and step through each action, or <code>--ui</code> for UI mode with a timeline and watch mode. Add <code>page.pause()</code> to stop at one line. Python and Java open the Inspector when the PWDEBUG=1 environment variable is set. The VS Code extension lets you run, debug and pick locators from the editor.',
      '<p><code>--headed</code> shows the browser, and <code>slowMo</code> in the launch options slows each action so you can follow it.</p>'),
    Q('reports', 'Debugging and reports', 'm',
      'Which reports have you used with Playwright in each language?',
      'Name the built in reporters, then the common third party ones.',
      'Playwright Test has built in reporters such as list, line, dot, html, json, junit and blob, and several can run at once. Python teams use pytest-html or allure-pytest. Java teams use the TestNG reports, Extent Reports through a listener, or Allure. Allure has adapters for all of them and shows history, steps and attachments.',
      '<p>CI tools read the JUnit XML to show pass and fail counts, while people read the HTML or Allure report. Screenshots, videos and traces of failed tests should be attached to the report.</p>',
      follow=['How do you attach a screenshot to Extent or Allure when a test fails?']),
    Q('screenshot-failure', 'Debugging and reports', 'm',
      'How do you capture a screenshot only when a test fails?',
      'Config in TypeScript and Python, a listener in Java.',
      'Playwright Test: <code>screenshot: "only-on-failure"</code> and <code>video: "retain-on-failure"</code> in the use block of config. Python: <code>--screenshot only-on-failure</code> and <code>--video retain-on-failure</code>. Java with TestNG: an ITestListener whose <code>onTestFailure</code> calls <code>page.screenshot()</code> and attaches the bytes to the report.',
      '<p>TestNG calls <code>onTestFailure</code> before <code>@AfterMethod</code>, so the page is still open when the listener takes the screenshot.</p>'),
    Q('visual', 'Debugging and reports', 'h',
      'How does visual comparison work, and what makes it flaky?',
      'A first run writes the baseline. Fonts and machines differ.',
      '<code>await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot()</code> compares the page with a saved baseline image and fails when pixels differ beyond the allowed threshold. The first run writes the baseline. It is a Playwright Test feature in TypeScript and JavaScript. Screenshots differ between operating systems and browsers because fonts and rendering differ, so teams create and compare baselines in the same Docker image.',
      '<p>Mask changing parts such as dates with the <code>mask</code> option, and update baselines with <code>--update-snapshots</code> after a change is reviewed.</p>'),

    # ---------------- CI and scale ----------------
    Q('parallel', 'CI and scale', 'm',
      'How does Playwright run tests in parallel in each language?',
      'Workers in TypeScript, xdist in Python, threads in Java.',
      'Playwright Test runs test files in parallel worker processes, set with <code>workers</code>, and <code>fullyParallel: true</code> also runs tests inside one file in parallel. Python uses pytest-xdist, for example <code>pytest -n auto</code>. Java uses the TestNG <code>parallel</code> and <code>thread-count</code> settings, or JUnit parallel execution, with one Playwright object per thread.',
      '<p>Parallel runs only work when tests are independent, each with its own context and its own data.</p>'),
    Q('sharding', 'CI and scale', 'h',
      'What is sharding, and how do you get one report from four shards?',
      'Split across machines, then merge.',
      'Sharding splits the suite across machines: <code>npx playwright test --shard=1/4</code> on the first machine, 2/4 on the second and so on. Each shard writes a blob report, the pipeline uploads them as artifacts, and a final job downloads them all and runs <code>npx playwright merge-reports --reporter html ./all-blob-reports</code>.',
      '<p>Python teams often use pytest-split to share tests across machines. Java teams split by TestNG suite files or by package.</p>'),
    Q('retries-flaky', 'CI and scale', 'm',
      'What does Playwright mark as flaky, and how do you deal with flaky tests?',
      'A test that fails, then passes on retry.',
      'With retries on, a test that fails and then passes on a retry is reported as flaky. Retries keep the pipeline moving, but each flaky test needs a cause: usually a missing wait for the right thing, shared data between tests, or order dependence. Look at the trace from the failed attempt to find it.',
      '<p>Playwright Test sets retries in config, often <code>retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0</code>. Python uses pytest-rerunfailures with <code>--reruns 2</code>. Java uses a TestNG IRetryAnalyzer.</p>',
      follow=['What was the last flaky test you fixed, and what was the cause?']),
    Q('ci-pipeline', 'CI and scale', 'm',
      'What does a Playwright job in GitHub Actions need?',
      'Code, the runtime, dependencies, browsers, the run, the report.',
      'Check out the code, set up the runtime (Node, Python or Java), install dependencies with a lock file (<code>npm ci</code>, <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code> or Maven), install the browsers with <code>--with-deps</code>, run the tests, and upload the report and traces as an artifact with <code>if: always()</code> so they are kept when tests fail.',
      '<p>Store passwords as repository secrets and pass them in as environment variables. GitHub Actions and GitLab set <code>CI=true</code>, which configs read to change retries, workers and <code>forbidOnly</code>.</p>'),
    Q('docker', 'CI and scale', 'h',
      'Why run Playwright tests in the official Docker image?',
      'Same browsers and system libraries on every machine.',
      'The image from mcr.microsoft.com/playwright comes with the browsers and the system libraries they need for one Playwright version, so CI and every developer run the same setup. Pick the image tag that matches the Playwright version in your project. Run it with <code>--ipc=host</code> for Chromium and <code>--init</code> so stray processes are cleaned up.',
      '<p>There are images for Node, Python (mcr.microsoft.com/playwright/python) and Java (mcr.microsoft.com/playwright/java). Matching images also keep visual baselines stable.</p>'),
    Q('test-only', 'CI and scale', 'e',
      'Someone pushed <code>test.only</code> by mistake. How do you stop that from passing CI?',
      'There is a config option for exactly this.',
      'Set <code>forbidOnly: !!process.env.CI</code> in playwright.config.ts. In CI any <code>test.only</code> then fails the run instead of quietly running just one test. A lint rule from eslint-plugin-playwright can catch it before the push too.',
      '<p>Python and Java runners do not have <code>test.only</code>, so the same mistake there is a narrowed run command, such as a <code>-k</code> filter or a <code>-Dtest</code> option left in a CI script. Review pipeline changes for that.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Newer features ----------------
    Q('agents', 'New features and AI', 'm',
      'What are the Playwright Test Agents?',
      'Three agents, one command to add them.',
      'Since version 1.56 Playwright ships three agent definitions for AI coding assistants: a planner that explores the app and writes a Markdown test plan, a generator that turns the plan into tests and checks locators against the live app, and a healer that runs a failing test and fixes it or skips it. You add them with <code>npx playwright init-agents --loop=claude</code> (or vscode, codex, opencode and others), which also writes a seed test and an MCP config.',
      '<p>The agents write Playwright Test code in TypeScript or JavaScript. A person still reviews every plan and test before it is merged.</p>'),
    Q('mcp', 'New features and AI', 'm',
      'What is the Playwright MCP server, and how is it different from the test agents?',
      'One lets any assistant drive a browser. The other writes tests.',
      'The Playwright MCP server lets an AI assistant that supports the Model Context Protocol control a browser through tools such as navigate, click and snapshot. It describes the page through its accessibility tree rather than screenshots. You add it with <code>npx @playwright/mcp@latest</code> in the assistant config. The test agents are built on a separate test MCP server and focus on planning, writing and fixing Playwright tests.',
      '<p>Python and Java teams can use the MCP server while an assistant writes pytest or Java tests, since it only drives the browser and does not care which language the tests are in.</p>'),
    Q('upgrade', 'New features and AI', 'e',
      'How do you upgrade Playwright safely in a team?',
      'A branch, the release notes, the browsers and CI.',
      'Read the release notes for breaking changes, update on a branch, update the package (npm, pip or the version in pom.xml), install the matching browsers, update the Docker image tag if CI uses one, and let the full suite run in CI before merging.',
      '<p>Each release lists removed APIs and changes in browser versions, which can also change screenshots used in visual tests.</p>'),

    # ---------------- Scenarios ----------------
    Q('slow-suite', 'Scenarios', 'h',
      'Your suite of 600 UI tests takes 90 minutes. How would you bring it down?',
      'Less work per test, more tests at once, fewer tests in the UI.',
      'First measure where time goes, using the report or the slowest tests list. Then log in once with storageState, create test data through the API, remove fixed sleeps, run tests in parallel with more workers, and shard across machines in CI. Finally move checks that do not need a browser, such as validation rules, down to API or unit tests.',
      '<p>Run a small smoke suite on every push and the full regression on a schedule or before release, so the slow run does not block each change.</p>',
      kind='Scenario'),
    Q('passes-local', 'Scenarios', 'm',
      'A test passes on your laptop and fails in CI. How do you investigate?',
      'Get the evidence from CI first, then compare the two machines.',
      'Open the trace, screenshot and video from the failed CI run to see what the page showed. Then compare the differences between the machines: headless against headed, screen size, speed, time zone and locale, data in the CI environment, and secrets that may be missing. Reproduce with the same Docker image and <code>CI=true</code> locally if needed.',
      '<p>Common causes are a missing wait that only shows on a slower machine, data left by another test, and a viewport that hides a menu behind a button.</p>',
      kind='Scenario'),
    Q('captcha-otp', 'Scenarios', 'm',
      'The login page has a CAPTCHA and an OTP sent by SMS. How do you automate tests behind it?',
      'You do not solve them. You agree a way around them with the team.',
      'Do not try to beat a CAPTCHA in tests. Ask the developers to disable it, or use a test key, in the test environment. For OTP use a test account with a fixed code, read the code from a test API or mail inbox, or log in through the API and load the session with storageState. Keep one manual or monitored check that the real flow works in production.',
      '<p>A CAPTCHA exists to stop automation, so a test that solves it would also show the CAPTCHA does not work. The usual answer in interviews is to agree a test only bypass with the developers.</p>',
      kind='Scenario'),
]
